{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\n56% of sampled fixes add a new mechanism (lock, knob, env var, gate script, fixture, helper, flag) vs 30% pure logic fixes (R2S1) — the surface that produces future defect classes grows with every fix cycle. Confirmed genuinely new (12a: no unified detector exists); per-kind precedents prove detectability (`check_config_reads.py` AST detector, `gen_env_registry.py`). Baseline keyed per `(kind, name)` — never a scalar sum — per the ESLint `eslint-suppressions.json` shape (12c), so one kind cannot be traded against another. 2026 grounding (12b): counter-pressure to general mechanism entropy (toggle-mining: deletions lag additions 13–35%), not an AI-specific premise.\n\n## Approach\n\n`scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py` detects, per repo STATE (not per diff — state-diffing the baseline is simpler and equivalent), seven mechanism kinds.\n\n**The seven kinds PARTITION the mechanism surface: every definition site produces exactly ONE `(kind, name)` entry.** This is a correctness requirement, not a tidiness one. A naive reading counts a boolean `rebar.toml` key twice — once as `config_key` and once as `feature_flag` — because the flag detector reads the same `_SECTIONS` dict. One definition site would then create two baseline entries and require two justifications, and a per-kind marker could not express that. The partition removes the overlap at its source: `feature_flag` claims the boolean-coerced `_SECTIONS` entries, and `config_key` claims **only the non-boolean remainder**. Live split of the 103 keys today: 27 boolean (`feature_flag`) + 76 (`config_key`). A test asserts the seven detectors' name sets are pairwise disjoint, so a future detector cannot silently reintroduce double-counting.\n\nLive counts, which the baseline is locked to at introduction:\n\n| kind | detection | count |\n|---|---|---|\n| `lock` | AST `ClassDef` matching `.*Lock.*`, plus string literals matching `\\.lock$` | 26 |\n| `env_var` | reuse `gen_env_registry.scan()`, filtered to `REBAR_*` | 132 |\n| `config_key` | `_config_sections._SECTIONS`, **excluding** boolean-coerced entries | 76 |\n| `feature_flag` | `_SECTIONS` entries whose coercer is boolean (no dedicated flag registry exists) | 27 |\n| `ci_gate` | `scripts/check_*.py` glob, plus workflow `run:` steps | 280 (29 scripts + 251 run-steps) |\n| `autouse_fixture` | AST: `pytest.fixture(autouse=True)` decorators | 145 |\n| `test_helper` | `tests/_*.py` glob | 19 |\n\n**Baseline and modes.** `.github/mechanism-baseline.json`, shape `{\"schema_version\": 1, \"mechanisms\": {\"<kind>::<name>\": 1}}`, sorted keys, duplicate-member and UTF-8 rejection — a near-verbatim port of the complexity baseline's validated schema. The four-bucket `compare()` (new / increased / active / stale) and `has_regression = new or increased` are ported unchanged; they are 16 proven lines.\n\n`check_complexity_baseline.py` exposes **three** gate commands — `--check`, `--update-stale`, and `--lock` (a real, dispatched mode comparing the branch baseline against main's copy). This plan adopts `--check` and `--update-stale` and **deliberately omits `--lock`**: `--lock` exists because the complexity *threshold* is administrator-locked, so a contributor raising a ceiling must be caught cross-branch. The mechanism baseline carries no administrator-locked limit — a new mechanism is caught by `--check` on the branch itself — so `--lock` would add ~135 LOC of base-fetch machinery guarding nothing. If the baseline later gains a locked limit, `--lock` is the known port.\n\n**The justified-exception path is an in-tree marker, parsed by this script.** A new mechanism may be admitted by an inline comment at its definition site:\n\n```\n# mechanism-ok: <kind> <name> — <reason or ticket id>\n```\n\n`check_mechanism_delta.py` reads these markers during its own per-state scan, exactly as `check_config_reads.py` consumes its own `# read-via:` markers, and a marker with a blank reason is itself an error. Because the kinds partition, one site needs exactly one marker naming one kind.\n\n**Where the marker attaches, per kind.** Three of the seven kinds are detected by filename glob or by YAML structure and so have no single definition line the way an AST `ClassDef` or a `_SECTIONS` entry does. The rule is therefore stated per detection shape, and each detector module scans exactly the locations named here:\n\n| detection shape | kinds | marker location scanned |\n|---|---|---|\n| a Python definition line | `lock` (class), `autouse_fixture`, `config_key`, `feature_flag` | on the definition line, or the line immediately preceding it — the `# read-via:` discipline verbatim |\n| a string literal | `lock` (`*.lock` filenames), `env_var` | on the literal's line, or the line immediately preceding it, in any file the detector attributes the name to |\n| a filename glob | `ci_gate` (`scripts/check_*.py`), `test_helper` (`tests/_*.py`) | anywhere in the first 20 lines of the matched file, so a new gate script declares itself in its own header |\n| a YAML step | `ci_gate` (workflow `run:` steps) | on the step's `- name:`/`run:` line, or the line immediately preceding it |\n\nA mechanism attributed to several files is admitted by a marker in any one of them; the diagnostic names every attributed file so the author knows where a marker would count. An earlier draft routed this through \"the DET floor\"; that was wrong and is dropped — the DET floor (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_floor.py`, P1–P11) is the per-ticket plan-review gate dispatched by the orchestrator over a ticket description, is not wired into `make lint`, and cannot be consulted by a static script scanning the repo tree. The in-tree marker also satisfies the greppable/countable requirement that a per-ticket justification could not.\n\n**Structure.** Seven detectors in one file would breach both the 800-LOC module cap and the complexity-15 ceiling, so the work is a package: `scripts/_mechanism_delta/{baseline,compare,detect_code,detect_config,detect_ci}.py` with a thin entrypoint. Detectors split by INPUT SURFACE (Python AST / config registry / globs+YAML), and dispatch is a flat `{kind: callable}` table, keeping the entrypoint at complexity ~4.\n\nPortable by construction: stdlib plus PyYAML, triggered by `make lint` (and thus pre-commit and the Verified lane), with no CI-provider dependency.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: the seven detectors' name sets are pairwise disjoint on the current tree. Verified by a test asserting every pair has an empty intersection.\n- [ ] AC2: `config_key` excludes boolean-coerced `_SECTIONS` entries. Verified by a test asserting its live count is 76.\n- [ ] AC3: `feature_flag` claims exactly those boolean-coerced entries. Verified by a test asserting its live count is 27.\n- [ ] AC4: the script detects all seven kinds with zero false positives on the current tree. Verified by `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exiting 0 on a clean tree.\n- [ ] AC5: `--check` exits non-zero when a new `(kind, name)` appears without justification. Verified by a per-kind parameterized test seeding one new mechanism of each of the seven kinds.\n- [ ] AC6: a new mechanism carrying a valid `# mechanism-ok:` marker passes. Verified by a test seeding a marked mechanism of a Python-definition-line kind.\n- [ ] AC7: the marker is honored for a filename-glob kind. Verified by a test seeding a new `scripts/check_*.py` whose marker sits in its first 20 lines.\n- [ ] AC8: the marker is honored for a YAML-step kind. Verified by a test seeding a new workflow `run:` step with a marker on the preceding line.\n- [ ] AC9: a marker whose reason is blank fails. Verified by a test seeding a blank-reason marker.\n- [ ] AC10: mechanism removal passes with shrink-only semantics. Verified by a test removing a baselined mechanism and asserting exit 0.\n- [ ] AC11: `--update-stale` refuses to write when `new>0` or `increased>0`. Verified by a test asserting the refusal and that the baseline file is byte-identical afterwards.\n- [ ] AC12: `make lint` runs the check. Verified by a test asserting the Makefile `lint` target contains the invocation.\n- [ ] AC13: the ratchet and its seven kinds are documented where the complexity ratchet is documented. Verified by a test asserting the docs section names all seven kind identifiers.\n- [ ] AC14: the marker discipline, including the per-detection-shape placement table, is documented in that same section. Verified by a test asserting the docs section names each of the four detection shapes.\n- [ ] AC15: every shipped module stays within the 800-LOC cap. Verified by the module-size gate.\n- [ ] AC16: every shipped function stays within the complexity-15 ceiling. Verified by `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exiting 0.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py -v` — the suite covering AC1, AC2, AC4–AC10. Census counts are derived LIVE from the tree, never hardcoded, following `tests/unit/test_complexity_baseline.py`'s stated discipline.\n- `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` — exits 0 on the clean tree (AC3).\n- `make lint` — runs the new gate beside `check_complexity_baseline.py --check` (AC9) and proves the additions clear the complexity ratchet (AC11).\n- `make typecheck` — the package type-checks.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py` (new; entrypoint plus dispatch table)\n- `scripts/_mechanism_delta/` (new package: `baseline.py`, `compare.py`, `detect_code.py`, `detect_config.py`, `detect_ci.py`)\n- `.github/mechanism-baseline.json` (new; per-`(kind, name)` baseline locked at introduction)\n- `Makefile` (lint wiring, after `check_complexity_baseline.py --check`)\n- `tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py` (new; the direct precedent `test_complexity_baseline.py` lives in `tests/unit/`, not `tests/scripts/`)\n- `docs/` (contributor-facing ratchet note beside the complexity ratchet)"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788319390120975001,"uuid":"938c3187-154a-434a-80ac-5a458bea1e0f"}